/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The true value of telemedicine lies in the convenience it provides to both patients and healthcare providers. It eliminates the need for in-person visits for medical advice or treatment and is often more cost-effective than traditional consultations. This efficiency has made telemedicine an essential component of modern healthcare.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
421 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Telemedicine comes in several forms. These include real-time communication through video calls; remote monitoring, which involves collecting, reporting, and evaluating health data such as blood pressure, cardiac activity, oxygen levels, and respiratory rates; and store-and-forward technology, which allows medical information like CAT scans, MRIs, X-rays, photos, videos, and text-based patient data to be stored and shared for later evaluation.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
422 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Despite its many advantages, telemedicine also has important disadvantages. Limited internet access and unreliable connectivity can prevent patients from receiving consistent virtual care. Technical issues may interrupt consultations or reduce the quality of communication between patient and provider. Some medical concerns still require physical examinations or hands-on procedures, making telemedicine insufficient in certain cases. In addition, concerns about data privacy and cybersecurity continue to arise, as the storage and transfer of digital medical information may pose risks if systems are not adequately protected.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
